[2026-09-08T23:11:25] Q3: How does the paper measure whether library learning compression improves on DreamCoder?
retrieved=['DBLP_journals_pacmpl_BowersOWGTES23#chunk71 (35.78)', 'DBLP_journals_pacmpl_BowersOWGTES23#chunk56 (35.19)', 'DBLP_journals_corr_abs-2006-08381#chunk30 (32.89)']
grading_notes=Must mention comparing rewritten corpus size / compression ratio between Stitch and DreamCoder (Eq. 18 / best-of-task metric).
verdict=pass
answer=The paper measures this using a **compression metric** based on the **rewritten corpus size ratio** between DreamCoder and Stitch.

It says they compare library quality with:

> “the compression metric in Eq. (18)”  
> and show “**Rewritten corpus size ratio (DreamCoder / Stitch)** … Higher is better for Stitch: a ratio above 1.0 indicates that Stitch achieves greater compression than DreamCoder.”

The metric is defined as:

> “\(\sum\) task cost(A) + min cost(Rewrite(p, A))”  
> (Eq. 18)

and they use the “**best-of-task**” version in Stitch:

> “it is trivial to implement this best-of-task metric in Stitch, so we use this for the comparisons with DreamCoder.”

So, in short, they judge whether compression improves on DreamCoder by comparing the **compression score / rewritten corpus size ratio**; values above 1.0 mean Stitch compresses better than DreamCoder.
